        "delegation_parent": "R/A",
        "depth": 1,
        "epoch": 1,
        "hypothesis": "Contiguous input-order adjacency lists and one-time ID resolution should reduce scattered linear scans and repeated tokenization. Sequential neighbor passes and cached iterative path evaluation may also improve locality and avoid recomputing shared paths. The baseline is only about 12 ms, so the speed benefit is an unmeasured hypothesis.",
        "id": "bbf84f6d35e84d84",
        "ordinal": 1,
        "parent_id": null,
        "role": "A",
        "source_commits": [],
        "sources": [],
        "transformations": {
          "files": [
            "src/graph.c"
          ],
          "retained": [
            "Parse graph records into compact node metadata and contiguous adjacency storage, then resolve neighbor IDs once through an open-addressed first-match map while retaining input order.",
            "Preselect each node's neighbor using the original zero initial maximum, strict greater-than comparison, first-on-ties behavior, missing-ID handling, and -1 recursion sentinel.",
            "Replace repeated recursive recomputation with demand-driven memoized evaluation over an explicit path stack; preserve root tokens and six-decimal output formatting."
          ]
        },
